### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer is testing humility, honesty, and the ability to extract durable lessons. A defensive answer that blames others signals someone who will not learn. An honest answer that says "I missed this, here is why, and here is what I always do now" signals continuous growth.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class FailureTest {
    enum Signal { GROWTH, DEFENSIVE }
    public static Signal evaluate(boolean ownedIt, boolean learnedLesson, boolean builtSystem) {
        return (ownedIt && learnedLesson && builtSystem) ? Signal.GROWTH : Signal.DEFENSIVE;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true, true)); // GROWTH
    }
}
```

### What was the genuine failure?

I deployed a change to the fee calculation in the transfer service at National Bank of India without a safe rollback path. A subtle bug double-charged the fee on some of Priya's transfers, and because I had no quick way to revert, the bad version stayed live longer than it should have.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class TheFailure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("What: Deployed fee change with no rollback plan");
        System.out.println("Bug: Double-charged fee on some transfers");
        System.out.println("Impact: Bad version live for 45 minutes instead of seconds");
        System.out.println("My fault: I focused on shipping, not on what-if-it-breaks");
    }
}
```

### How did you take ownership?

I did not blame the deadline or the requirements. The truth was that I shipped a money-handling change with no safety net, and in a bank that is a serious lapse.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class FullOwnership {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Wrong: 'The deadline forced me to skip the rollback plan'");
        System.out.println("Right: 'I chose to ship without a rollback plan. That was my mistake.'");
    }
}
```

### What was the root cause?

The root cause was not the bug itself (bugs are inevitable) but the absence of a safe deployment process. No health check, no automatic rollback, no canary.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class RootCause {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Surface cause: subtle fee calculation bug");
        System.out.println("Real cause: deployment had no health check or auto-rollback");
        System.out.println("Lesson: 'be more careful' is not a system; auto-rollback is");
    }
}
```

### What systemic change did you build?

I built a deployment flow where an automated health check verifies the new version right after deployment, and if the check fails, the system reverts to the previous known-good version in seconds.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class AutoRollback {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(AutoRollback.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean healthCheckPassed = false; // simulating failure
        if (!healthCheckPassed) {
            log.warning("Health check FAILED: auto-rolling back to previous version");
            log.info("Rollback complete in 8 seconds");
        } else {
            log.info("Health check PASSED: new version is live");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Health check FAILED: auto-rolling back to previous version
Rollback complete in 8 seconds
```

### How did the lesson stick?

After the auto-rollback system was in place, a bad deploy went from an hours-long incident to a seconds-long blip. The team adopted the same pattern across other services.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class LessonStuck {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Before: bad deploy = hours-long incident");
        System.out.println("After: bad deploy = seconds-long auto-rollback");
        System.out.println("Team adoption: all services now use the same pattern");
    }
}
```

### What failures should you avoid naming?

Do not use a disguised brag ("I worked too hard"), a trivial failure ("I was late to a meeting once"), or someone else's failure ("my teammate dropped the ball").

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class BadFailureChoices {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Bad: 'I worked too hard' -> disguised brag");
        System.out.println("Bad: 'I was late once' -> trivial, not meaningful");
        System.out.println("Bad: 'My teammate messed up' -> blaming others");
        System.out.println("Good: 'I shipped without a rollback plan' -> real, owned, systemic fix");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "Has it happened again?" (no, the auto-rollback prevents it), "What would you do differently?" (build the rollback first, before shipping), and "How did the team react?" (they adopted the pattern).

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class FailureFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Again? -> No, auto-rollback catches bad deploys in seconds");
        System.out.println("Differently? -> Build rollback mechanism before shipping any change");
        System.out.println("Team? -> Adopted auto-rollback across all services");
    }
}
```
